---
/**
 * Animated GA4 data flow, from every kind of source to every destination.
 *
 * Sources: web (page → dataLayer → gtag.js / GTM), apps (Firebase SDK), and
 * TV / OTT / servers (Measurement Protocol), with optional server-side GTM.
 * Then collection → property processing (each step clickable) → reporting,
 * ad platforms, BigQuery export → warehouses, plus data import flowing in.
 *
 * Web packets are real: every gtag() call on the page dispatches `ga4:hit`.
 * App and TV traffic is simulated. Downstream stages batch on a compressed
 * clock (1 s ≈ 1 h).
 */
const stages = {
  ui: {
    title: 'Page and UI',
    body: 'A click, view, scroll, or form submit on a web page. Tagging code (or a GTM trigger) reacts by calling gtag() with an event name and parameters. Enhanced measurement adds page_view, scroll, outbound click, site search, video, and file download events without any code.',
  },
  datalayer: {
    title: 'dataLayer',
    body: 'gtag() and GTM both push onto window.dataLayer, a plain array that works as a queue: anything pushed before the library loads is replayed when it does, so no early event is lost. GTM reads the same queue to fire tags.',
  },
  sdk: {
    title: 'gtag.js / Google Tag Manager',
    body: 'Reads the queue and adds the client ID (_ga cookie), session ID and count (_ga_<ID> cookie), page location, and consent state. Events are batched for a moment and sent with sendBeacon or fetch. If analytics consent is denied (Consent Mode), it sends cookieless pings with no identifiers instead.',
  },
  app: {
    title: 'Firebase SDK (iOS / Android apps)',
    body: 'Apps use the Google Analytics for Firebase SDK instead of gtag.js. It identifies the install with an app_instance_id, logs automatic events (first_open, session_start, screen_view, in_app_purchase, app_update), and stores events on the device. Batches upload to app-measurement.com about once an hour, or sooner when the app goes to the background, so app data arrives in bursts and survives being offline.',
  },
  mp: {
    title: 'Measurement Protocol (TV, OTT, servers)',
    body: "Devices with no Google SDK (Roku, smart TVs, game consoles, kiosks) and back-end systems (refunds, subscription renewals, call-center sales) send events as JSON over HTTPS to /mp/collect with an api_secret. You must supply the client_id or app_instance_id and session_id yourself, so these hits only join a user's journey if your IDs match what the device or browser uses.",
  },
  sgtm: {
    title: 'Server-side GTM (optional)',
    body: 'A tagging server you run on your own subdomain (for example on Cloud Run). The browser sends GA4 requests to it in the same /g/collect format; it can drop or hash personal data, add server-only data, set first-party cookies, and forward the hit to GA4 and to other vendors. Toggle "via sGTM" to route web hits through it.',
  },
  collect: {
    title: 'Collection endpoints',
    body: 'Three front doors, all answered by the nearest Google edge: /g/collect for web, app-measurement.com/a for the Firebase SDK, and /mp/collect for the Measurement Protocol. They reply 204 with no body and do not validate anything, so a misspelled parameter is accepted silently. Use DebugView, Tag Assistant, or the validation endpoint /debug/mp/collect while building.',
  },
  processing: {
    title: 'GA4 property processing',
    body: 'One property collects many data streams (a website, an iOS app, an Android app) so the same person can be counted once across all of them. Click any step inside the box to see what it does. Most standard reports finish processing within a day, sometimes up to 48 hours.',
  },
  'p-identity': {
    title: 'Identity',
    body: 'Reporting identity decides who a "user" is. Blended identity uses your user_id when someone signs in, falls back to the device (client ID on web, app_instance_id in apps), then to modeling for consent-denied traffic. Sending user_id is what lets web, app, and TV activity join into one person.',
  },
  'p-session': {
    title: 'Sessions',
    body: 'Events are grouped into sessions using the session ID set by the client. A session ends after 30 minutes of inactivity (configurable). Engaged sessions are those over 10 seconds, with a key event, or with two or more page or screen views; engagement rate is built from them.',
  },
  'p-attribution': {
    title: 'Attribution',
    body: 'Assigns traffic source (utm parameters, gclid auto-tagging, referrer) to each user and session, groups sources into default channels, and credits key events to channels with data-driven or last-click models over a lookback window (30 to 90 days).',
  },
  'p-key': {
    title: 'Key events',
    body: 'Events you mark as important (purchase, sign_up, generate_lead) are counted as key events, formerly called conversions. They drive attribution reports and can be imported into Google Ads as conversions for bidding.',
  },
  'p-audiences': {
    title: 'Audiences',
    body: 'Users who match conditions (watched 3+ videos, abandoned cart, high predicted purchase probability) are added to audiences as their events arrive. Audiences can trigger audience events and, with a Google Ads or DV360 link, are shared for remarketing.',
  },
  'p-enrich': {
    title: 'Geo and device',
    body: 'Country, region, and city come from the IP address, which GA4 does not log or store. Browser, OS, device category, and screen size come from the user agent and client hints, or from the SDK in apps.',
  },
  'p-filters': {
    title: 'Filters',
    body: 'Known bots and spiders are excluded automatically. Data filters you configure remove internal traffic (by IP rule) and developer traffic (debug_mode). Filtered data is gone permanently, so filters are usually tested in "Testing" state first.',
  },
  'p-consent': {
    title: 'Consent modeling',
    body: 'When Consent Mode reports analytics storage denied, GA4 gets cookieless pings. With enough traffic, behavioral modeling estimates the users and key events those pings represent and blends them into reports.',
  },
  'p-thresholds': {
    title: 'Custom definitions and thresholds',
    body: 'Event and user parameters only appear in reports once registered as custom dimensions or metrics, and only from that point forward. Thresholds hide rows with very few users when demographic or signals data is involved, to protect privacy. Explorations then read data within your retention window (2 or 14 months).',
  },
  import: {
    title: 'Data import and product links',
    body: 'Data flows in as well as out: cost data from non-Google ad platforms, CRM and user data keyed by user_id, item metadata, and offline events can be uploaded by file or SFTP. Linked Google Ads, Search Console, Merchant Center, and BigQuery accounts exchange data with the property.',
  },
  reporting: {
    title: 'Reporting',
    body: 'Standard reports, Explorations, and the Data API (which Looker Studio and other BI tools use) read the processed tables. Realtime shows the last 30 minutes and DebugView shows events from debug devices within seconds, both through a fast path that skips most processing.',
  },
  ads: {
    title: 'Ad platforms',
    body: 'With product links, audiences are shared to Google Ads, Display & Video 360, and Search Ads 360 for targeting, and key events are imported as conversions so bidding optimizes toward them. Ads cost and click data flows back into GA4 acquisition reports.',
  },
  bigquery: {
    title: 'BigQuery export',
    body: 'Raw, event-level rows in your own Google Cloud project: a daily events_YYYYMMDD table after the day closes, an optional streaming events_intraday_ table within minutes, and user tables (pseudonymous_users_, users_). No sampling or thresholds apply. Standard properties can export up to 1 million events a day.',
  },
  warehouse: {
    title: 'Warehouses and BI',
    body: 'Scheduled loads copy BigQuery tables onward to Snowflake, Databricks, Redshift, or a data lake, where they join CRM, ad spend, and product data. BI tools read from there, and reverse-ETL jobs push modeled segments back into CRM and ad platforms.',
  },
};

const procSteps = [
  ['p-identity', 'identity: user_id › device'],
  ['p-session', 'sessions (30 min timeout)'],
  ['p-attribution', 'attribution + channels'],
  ['p-key', 'key events'],
  ['p-audiences', 'audiences'],
  ['p-enrich', 'geo + device'],
  ['p-filters', 'filters: bots, internal, dev'],
  ['p-consent', 'consent modeling'],
  ['p-thresholds', 'custom definitions, thresholds'],
];
---
<figure class="ga4flow" data-ga4flow aria-labelledby="ga4flow-caption">
  <div class="ga4flow__bar">
    <span class="ga4flow__kicker">How GA4 data travels</span>
    <span class="ga4flow__clock" data-clock>day 1 · 00:00</span>
    <span class="ga4flow__spacer"></span>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="web">Web hit</button>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="app">App batch</button>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="mp">TV / server hit</button>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="sgtm" aria-pressed="false">via sGTM</button>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="auto" aria-pressed="true">Auto traffic</button>
    <button type="button" class="ga4flow__btn" data-action="pause" aria-pressed="false">Pause</button>
  </div>

  <div class="ga4flow__scroll">
  <svg class="ga4flow__svg" viewBox="0 0 1200 600" role="img" aria-labelledby="ga4flow-title ga4flow-desc">
    <title id="ga4flow-title">GA4 data flow</title>
    <desc id="ga4flow-desc">Three kinds of sources send events to Google Analytics 4: websites through the dataLayer and gtag.js or Google Tag Manager, mobile apps through the Firebase SDK, and TVs, OTT devices, and servers through the Measurement Protocol. Web hits can pass through an optional server-side GTM container. All arrive at collection endpoints, then the GA4 property processes them: identity, sessions, attribution, key events, audiences, geo and device, filters, consent modeling, and custom definitions. Processed data feeds reporting, ad platforms, and the BigQuery export, which loads onward to warehouses and BI tools. A realtime path goes from collection straight to reporting, and data import flows into processing.</desc>
    <defs>
      <marker id="ga4flow-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
        <path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="ga4flow__arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

